==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|coroner}}, from {{der|en|fro|curuner}}, from {{der|en|ML.|[[custōs]] [[placitum|placitōrum]] [[corōna]]e||guardian of the crown's pleas}}. The function was originally to protect royal properties.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɒ.ɹə.nə(ɹ)/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɔɹ.ə.nɚ/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɑɹ.ə.nɚ/|a=NYC}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɹaʊnə(ɹ)/|a=obsolete}} {{q|see {{m|en|crowner#Etymology 2}}}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-coroner.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Wodencafe-coroner.wav|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒɹənə(ɹ)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|Commonwealth|Japan|legal}} A [[public]] [[official]] who presides over an [[inquest]] into [[unnatural]] [[death]]s, and who may have (or historically had) additional powers such as investigating cases of [[treasure trove]].
# {{lb|en|Canada|US|medicine}} A [[medical doctor]] who performs [[autopsies]] and determines time and cause of [[death]] from a scientific standpoint.
# {{lb|en|Isle of Man}} The [[administrative]] [[head]] of a [[sheading]].

